"Let $X$ be a finite set, let $k\\in\\mathbb{N}$ satisfy $k\\leq\\log_L|X|$, and let $g\\colon{\\mleft(\\kern-.1em\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{X}{k}\\kern-.1em\\mright)}\\to[0,1]$. Define $\\left<g\\right>$ by\n\n$$\n\\left<g\\right>:=\\left\\{S\\in 2^X\\mathrel{\\left|}\\sum_{T\\subseteq S}g(T)\\geq 1\\right.\\right\\},\n$$\n\nand for a family $G\\subseteq 2^X$ define $w(G,p):=\\sum_{S\\in G}p^{|S|}$, with $w(g,p)$ defined analogously. **The bounded-uniformity conjecture.** There exists a fixed $L>1$ such that, for all finite sets $X$, all such $k$, all such functions $g$, and all $p\\in[0,1]$, if $w(g,p)=1$, then there exists a set $G\\subseteq2^X\\setminus\\{\\emptyset\\}$ such that $\\left<g\\right>\\subseteq\\left<G\\right>$ and\n\n$$\nw\\left(G,\\frac{p}{L}\\right)\\leq1.\n$$\n\nThis is a restricted form of Talagrand's conjecture whose resolution would imply the full conjecture. Results cited in the source cover constant-size support, and current quantitative bounds do not establish this claim in general."